 kinase 1 (Pak1) regulates motility in mammalian fibroblasts</article-title><source>J. Cell Biol.</source><volume>145</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>837</fpage><lpage>849 </lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10330410</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Sheetzetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sheetz</surname><given-names>M.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Felsenfeld</surname><given-names>D.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Galbraith</surname><given-names>C.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cell migrationregulation of force on extracellular matrix-integrin complexes</article-title><source>Trends Cell Biol.</source><volume>8</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>51</fpage><lpage>54 </lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9695809</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Tangetal1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tang</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>Z.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ambrose</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gibbs</surname><given-names>J.B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chernoff</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Field</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Kinase-deficient Pak1 mutants inhibit Ras transformation of Rat-1 fibroblasts</article-title><source>Mol. Cell. Biol.</source><volume>17</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>4454</fpage><lpage>4464 </lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9234703</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Warrenetal1993"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Warren</surname><given-names>K.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>J.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wamboldt</surname><given-names>D.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>J.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Overexpression of human fibroblast caldesmon fragment containing actin-, Ca<sup>2+</sup>/calmodulin-, and tropomyosin-binding domains stabilizes endogenous tropomyosin and microfilaments</article-title><source>J. Cell Biol.</source><volume>125</volume><year>1993</year><fpage>359</fpage><lpage>368 </lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8163552</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Westwicketal1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Westwick</surname><given-names>J.K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lambert</surname><given-names>Q.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Clark</surname><given-names>G.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Symons</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>VanAelst</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pestell</surname><given-names>R.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Der</surname><given-names>C.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Rac regulation of transformation, gene expression and actin organization by multiple, PAK-independent pathways</article-title><source>Mol. Cell. Biol.</source><volume>17</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>1324</fpage><lpage>1335 </lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9032259</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Zhangetal1995"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Han</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sells</surname><given-names>M.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chernoff</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Knaus</surname><given-names>U.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ulevitch</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bokoch</surname><given-names>G.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Rho family GTPases regulate p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase through the downstream mediator Pak1</article-title><source>J. Biol. Chem.</source><volume>270</volume><year>1995</year><fpage>23934</fpage><lpage>23936 </lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7592586</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Zhaoetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>Z.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Manser</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>X.Q.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chong</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Leung</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lim</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A conservative negative regulatory region in an alpha PAKinhibition of PAK kinases reveals their morphological roles downstream of Cdc42 and Rac1</article-title><source>Mol. Cell. Biol.</source><volume>18</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>2153</fpage><lpage>2163</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9528787</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> AC, active CAAX tagged&#x0003b; EC, endothelial cell&#x0003b; DN, dominant negative&#x0003b; FN, fibronectin&#x0003b; GFP, green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; HMEC, human microvascular endothelial cells&#x0003b; JNK, Jun kinase&#x0003b; MLC, myosin light chain&#x0003b; PAK, p21-activated kinase&#x0003b; P-MLC, phosphorylated myosin light chain&#x0003b; WT, wild-type.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Tracking migration. Time-lapse phase-contrast images acquired at 30-min intervals of the same EC that was outlined and tracked using the Inovision software. The white circle defines the position of the centroid and the arrows the translocation of the centroid from each successive time point. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>PAK activity assays. PAK was immunoprecipitated from lysates obtained from ECs that had been maintained in basal medium (Starved), stimulated with growth medium for 10 min (+ Serum), detached and kept in suspension in basal medium (Susp.), replated on FN for 10 min (Fn 10 min), or left stably attached in basal medium (Stably Att.). PAK kinase activity was assayed toward myelin basic protein. Top, PAK activity&#x0003b; bottom, Western blot of PAK protein in the immunoprecipitates.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Stimulation of migration by growth factors. Top, Rate of migration of uninjected ECs after plating on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml FN in the presence of growth medium (a) or basal medium (b), or 24 h after plating on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml FN in basal medium (c). Migration of cells injected with GFP alone was also determined after plating cells on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml FN in the presence of growth medium (d) and basal medium (e). Middle, Composite time-lapse phase-contrast images of cells, shown at 30-min intervals, in the conditions outlined above (labeled a&#x02013;e, respectively). Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m. Bottom, Rate of migration as a function of time after plating. Uninjected ECs were monitored after plating on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml FN in the presence of growth medium (a) and basal medium (b). Rate of migration was determined during each 30-min time interval.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Expression of PAK constructs. A Western blot showing expression of PAK constructs in COS-1 cells. Cells were transiently transfected using lipofectamine for 40 h with various constructs, lysed in PBS with 1% NP-40, and equal amounts of lysate were separated by SDS-PAGE, transferred to nitrocellulose membranes, and probed with myc antibody to recognize the tagged proteins (as described in Materials and Methods). Expressed proteins migrated at the expected molecular weights.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Effects of PAK constructs on cell migration. a, Diagrammatic representations of PAK constructs. Critical domains of the PAK constructs are indicated at the bottom of the figure and labeled proline-rich, GTPase binding domain, and kinase domain, accordingly. b, Rates of migration (mean &#x000b1; SD) of ECs plated on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml of FN in growth medium after injection with the constructs immediately to the left in a. c, Rates of migration (mean &#x000b1; SD) of ECs plated on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml of FN basal medium after injection with the constructs in the left in a.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Mapping the DN effect. a, Diagrammatic representations of PAK constructs. b, Rate of migration of ECs plated on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml of FN in the presence of growth medium after injection with the constructs shown immediately to the left in a. Values are mean &#x000b1; SD.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Morphology of migrating cells. Time-lapse series of phase-contrast micrographs showing 0 and 240 min, as indicated. The final panel outlines the composite phase-contrast images over the 4-h period. Cells were injected with GFP only (control), WT PAK, AC PAK, DN PAK, or the 1-74 PAK fragment. Expressing cells are indicated by arrows in each panel. Micrographs demonstrate similar levels of ruffling and lamellipodia, despite lack of migration in AC, DN, and 1-74 PAK.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Cytoskeletal structure. Fluorescence micrographs of ECs stained for actin and vinculin after injection with GFP alone, WT PAK, DN PAK, or AC PAK. Cells injected with DN and AC PAK show an increase in stress fibers and vinculin-containing focal contacts in comparison to GFP alone and WT PAK-injected ECs. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Cytoskeletal dynamics. Fluorescence images taken at 20-min intervals of ECs injected with GFP&#x02013;&#x003b1;-actinin alone (Control) or coinjected with GFP&#x02013;&#x003b1;-actinin plus WT PAK, AC PAK, or DN PAK. The first image in each set shows the entire cell (a). The next four images magnify the region within the box in a. The arrows in the magnified inserts for control and WT PAK show focal adhesions that disappear or grow and change shape. Bars, 20 &#x003bc;m. For AC PAK, the arrows identify an area where focal adhesions disappear and the cell retracts. For DN PAK, the upper arrow indicates a growing ruffle whereas the one below shows a stationary focal adhesion. Control and WT PAK-injected cells appear dynamic, whereas focal adhesions and stress fibers in cells injected with DN PAK are static. Cells injected with AC PAK are intermediate.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f9"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Cell tension on silicone rubber substrata. Phase-contrast images and corresponding fluorescence micrographs of ECs plated on 2 &#x003bc;g/ml FN coated onto flexible rubber substrates 4 h after injection with GFP alone, WT PAK, AC PAK, and DN PAK. Injected cells are labeled with an asterisk (*) in each phase-contrast panel. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m. The chart at the bottom shows the number of ECs that induced wrinkling of the substrate, and the corresponding percentages accumulated from five experiments.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f10"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F11"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>P-MLC staining fluorescence micrographs of ECs stained for actin and P-MLC after injection with WT PAK, DN PAK, or AC PAK. Cells injected with AC PAK show an increase in MLC staining in comparison to both DN and WT PAK-injected ECs. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m. Lower panel indicates quantitation of fluorescence intensity of P-MLC stained cells. Values represent mean &#x000b1; SEM from 15&#x02013;20 cells. Similar results were observed across four independent experiments.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f11"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F12"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Active PAK in NIH 3T3 fibroblasts. Fluorescence micrographs of NIH 3T3 fibroblasts stained for actin after injection with GFP and H83L,H86L PAK (left) or uninjected control cells (right). Cells injected with active PAK show increased lamellipodial formation and disruption of stress fibers within the lamellipodium (arrow). Bar, 15 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f12"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F13"><label>Figure 13</label><caption><p>A model for PAK in migration. A cartoon illustrates how PAK may coordinate EC migration. A cell migrates in the direction of predominant ruffling and lamellipodial extension (1). Extension of lamellipodia is followed by formation of new focal adhesions (2), leading to retraction of the tail of the cell at the trailing edge (3). We propose that PAK mediates the induction of tail retraction after formation of new adhesions. The black solid oval represents the nucleus, the bold lines represent stress fibers, and small outlined ovals represent focal adhesions (as indicated by the arrows). Dynamic events are shown as dotted lines.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904127.f13"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>